@namespace SpaceSails.Client.Pages
@implements IHandleEvent
@using SatchelPage = SpaceSails.Client.Pages.Map.SatchelPage
@*
    #251 · THE SATCHEL'S TAB BAR, ON ITS OWN FILE.

    THE POCKETS, AS A ROW. Four tabs that are always drawn and two that follow the world: SPREAD
    appears where there is a table under you and THE BIN where there is a bucket at your boots, because
    on your feet neither is a page you are being refused — it is a page that does not apply. Every count
    on them is the book's or the compass's own; this markup computes no badge.

    Moved out of SatchelPanel.razor by PURE MOTION — the block between the two fences below is the block
    that was in the panel, character for character. Every member it reads is a [Parameter] with the SAME
    NAME (a field keeps its underscore, a method arrives as a delegate under its own name, a variable the
    panel's `@if` bound arrives under the name that binding gave it), which is what lets the markup move
    without a rewrite and lets the composition splice it straight back where it came from.

    IHandleEvent repeats SatchelPanel's own override, which repeats Map.Sim.cs's: the HUD's refresh
    belongs to OnTick's 200 ms throttle, and a child that did not repeat it would put an automatic render
    back on every event in here. The .razor.css beside this file carries ONE of the four rule blocks
    that only this markup renders — the strip; the sheet itself says why the three `.satchel-tab`
    blocks could not follow it (#1166, and the cascade). Its scope is the PAGE's own (b-4dqsdx4p75,
    pinned in SpaceSails.Client.csproj), so every rule — the one beside it and the three left behind
    with the shared kit in SatchelPanel.razor.css — goes on matching this markup.

    The `@{ }` around the fences is not decoration: the block came out of a Razor CODE block, and a code
    block is where its first statements are legal. It is outside the fences, so what the composition reads
    is exactly the lines that stood in the panel.
*@
@{
@* ── MARKUP BEGINS · verbatim from SatchelPanel.razor ────────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
                @* #690 · A pocket and a notebook are both things a satchel holds. Owner: "should we have
                   notes / clues section in our inventory ui?" — and on the register: "it's like our detective
                   notepad :-D". The field book used to render only at a ship-brain desk, so the captain
                   standing at a sealed door could not consult their own notes; #688's leave verb made that
                   acute by filing a paper's gist to exactly that unreachable place. No second store here —
                   the NOTES page reads _fieldNotes, the one book. *@
                <div class="satchel-tabs">
                    <button type="button"
                            class="satchel-tab @(_satchelPage == SatchelPage.Carried ? "on" : "")"
                            title="What is in the satchel"
                            @onclick="() => _satchelPage = SatchelPage.Carried">
                        🎒 CARRIED@(_satchel.Count > 0 ? $" ({_satchel.Count})" : "")
                    </button>
                    <button type="button"
                            class="satchel-tab @(_satchelPage == SatchelPage.Notes ? "on" : "")"
                            title="What the ground has told you"
                            @onclick="() => _satchelPage = SatchelPage.Notes">
                        📓 NOTES
                    </button>
                    @* #741 v1 · THREADS, beside the book and reading the same one. Owner's issue: "a third
                       tab — THREADS — grouping existing entries by the entities they already name… the
                       THREAD is just the stack of what you wrote about one name, and the captain draws the
                       line." ALWAYS drawn, like the compass: an empty page here is an answer, and a tab
                       that appeared only once a case was forming would be missing exactly while a captain
                       wondered whether the game did this at all. The count is the book's, not a badge. *@
                    <button type="button"
                            class="satchel-tab @(_satchelPage == SatchelPage.Threads ? "on" : "")"
                            title="@SpaceSails.Core.CaseSubjects.TabHint"
                            @onclick="() => TheSatchelTurnsTo(SatchelPage.Threads)">
                        @SpaceSails.Core.CaseSubjects.TabLabel@(TheBookThreads() is { Count: > 0 } stacks
                            ? $" ({stacks.Count})" : "")
                    </button>
                    @* #727 · THE THIRD POCKET. What you learned (📓) and what you owe (🗺) are two different
                       questions, and the book keeps its opinionless voice by not being asked the second one.
                       Unlike SPREAD and THE BIN this tab is ALWAYS drawn: a compass that vanished when there
                       was nothing on it would answer "why am I down here" with silence, and the whole point
                       of the page is that the answer is never nothing — it is "nothing owed on foot", said
                       out loud, on the page. *@
                    <button type="button"
                            class="satchel-tab @(_satchelPage == SatchelPage.Missions ? "on" : "")"
                            title="What you took on, and what of it you can do standing here"
                            @onclick="() => _satchelPage = SatchelPage.Missions">
                        🗺 MISSIONS@(CompassOnFoot() is { Count: > 0 } owed ? $" ({owed.Count})" : "")
                    </button>
                    @* #784 · THE SPREAD, and the tab exists only when there is a table under it. Owner's law:
                       "writing things down requires sitting down to be properly done" — and the tab follows
                       the posture rather than standing there greyed out, because on your feet this is not a
                       page you are being refused, it is a page that does not apply. The refusal a captain
                       DOES need to meet (the bar desk) is inside the page, on the seat that has one. *@
                    @if (CaptainIsSeatedAnywhere)
                    {
                        <button type="button"
                                class="satchel-tab @(_satchelPage == SatchelPage.Spread ? "on" : "")"
                                title="@SpreadDoorHint"
                                @onclick="() => _satchelPage = SatchelPage.Spread">
                            🗂 SPREAD
                        </button>
                    }
                    @* #828 · THE BIN, and the tab follows the FIXTURE the way the spread tab follows the
                       posture: standing at a bucket it is a page, three du away it is not a page you are
                       being refused. It exists so the captain who opened the pocket by reflex ([I]) can
                       still find the bin they are standing at, and so the one who came in through [E] can
                       get back to their pockets and their book without walking away first. *@
                    @if (BinWithinReach() is { } binTab)
                    {
                        <button type="button"
                                class="satchel-tab @(_satchelPage == SatchelPage.Bin ? "on" : "")"
                                title="@SpaceSails.Core.RipAndBin.Hint(binTab.Tier)"
                                @onclick="() => _satchelPage = SatchelPage.Bin">
                            @SpaceSails.Core.RipAndBin.Glyph THE BIN
                        </button>
                    }
                </div>
@* ── MARKUP ENDS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
}
